 This example tests the "pjdos" capability of epsilon.x: a generalization
 of the joint density of states (jdos, calculation='jdos' in the INPUTPP
 namelist) in which every electronic transition i(occupied) -> j(empty)
 is weighted by the projection of the initial and final Kohn-Sham states
 onto a chosen set of atomic orbitals, instead of being counted with unit
 weight. The weights are read from two files whose names are given
 through the filproj1 (initial/occupied states) and filproj2
 (final/empty states) variables of the INPUTPP namelist.

 The test system is LiCoO2 treated with DFT+U+V, using the same SCF
 input as PW/examples/example13.

 1) make a self-consistent DFT+U+V calculation for LiCoO2
    (input=LiCoO2.scf.in, output=LiCoO2.scf.out)

 2) make a non-self-consistent calculation on the same k-point grid,
    adding empty bands (nbnd=40): jdos/pjdos need conduction states too
    (input=LiCoO2.nscf.in, output=LiCoO2.nscf.out)

 3) run projwfc.x to compute the projections of every Kohn-Sham state
    onto the atomic orbitals of the pseudopotentials, with
    diag_basis=.true.. With filproj set, projwfc.x writes, on top of
    the usual PDOS(E) files, the ASCII file 'LiCoO2.projwfc_up'
    containing, for every atomic wavefunction, the list of
    |<atomic wfc|psi_{ik,ibnd}>|^2 for every k-point and band
    (see PP/src/write_proj.f90::write_proj_file).
    diag_basis=.true. is needed because the CoO6 octahedra of this
    rhombohedral cell are NOT aligned with the global x,y,z axes: it
    rotates, independently for every atom, the atomic orbitals of each
    shell to the local basis that diagonalizes that atom's on-site
    occupation matrix (see PP/src/projwfc.f90::rotate_basis) -- for an
    octahedrally coordinated transition-metal ion this is the t2g/eg
    eigenbasis, regardless of how the octahedron happens to be oriented
    in the cell.
    (input=LiCoO2.projwfc.in, output=LiCoO2.projwfc.out)

 4) build the '*_up.proj' files read by epsilon.x (filproj1/filproj2)
    by summing the relevant atomic-wfc blocks of 'LiCoO2.projwfc_up'.
    This is done by the script ../extract_proj.sh, which greps the
    header line of each atomic-wfc block ("nwfc na atm els n l m") and
    sums, for every (ik,ibnd), the projections of the blocks matching a
    given list of atom indices, an orbital label, and a subset of the
    magnetic quantum number m (last column of the header line). Passing
    "all" for the m selection sums the whole shell (basis-independent,
    so it is the same with or without diag_basis); passing a subset,
    e.g. "3,4,5", isolates individual orbitals or symmetry-adapted
    combinations without summing over all m -- this is what makes it
    possible to inspect a specific d-d transition instead of the full,
    m-summed, d -> d jdos.

    Because of diag_basis, the Co-3D "m" index (1..5) no longer labels
    the fixed global-frame real harmonics (dz2,dzx,dzy,dx2-y2,dxy): it
    labels the eigenvectors of the local on-site occupation matrix, in
    ASCENDING eigenvalue order (QE's diagonalizer convention). For this
    system (see LiCoO2.projwfc.out, "ATOM 1" / "Orbital quantum number
    l = 2"), the eigenvalues are 0.740 0.740 1.981 1.981 1.982, a clean
    2+3 split: m=1,2 are the low-occupation eg orbitals, m=3,4,5 the
    high-occupation t2g orbitals (consistent with low-spin Co3+, d6,
    t2g^6 eg^0, softened by Co-O covalency). This m<->orbital
    correspondence is specific to this run and must be re-checked from
    the eigenvalues whenever the system changes.
      Co_d_up.proj    : sum of all 5 Co-3D orbitals (atom 1)
      Co_t2g_up.proj  : sum of the Co-3D t2g orbitals (m=3,4,5)
      Co_eg_up.proj   : sum of the Co-3D eg orbitals  (m=1,2)
      O_p_up.proj     : sum of the 2P orbitals of atoms 2 and 3 (both O)

 5) run epsilon.x three times with calculation='jdos':
      - without filproj1/filproj2  -> plain jdos
        (input=LiCoO2.jdos.in, output jdos_LiCoO2_plain.dat)
      - filproj1='O_p', filproj2='Co_d' -> pjdos weighted by O-2p
        character in the initial (occupied) states and Co-3d character
        in the final (empty) states, i.e. an O(2p) -> Co(3d)
        charge-transfer-like weighted jdos
        (input=LiCoO2.pjdos_Op_Cod.in, output pjdos_LiCoO2_Op_Cod.dat)
      - filproj1='Co_t2g', filproj2='Co_eg' -> pjdos for the Co(t2g) ->
        Co(eg) crystal-field d-d transition, obtained by selecting a
        subset of the m's of the Co-3d shell for the initial and final
        states respectively
        (input=LiCoO2.pjdos_t2g_eg.in, output pjdos_LiCoO2_t2g_eg.dat)

 The output files are 2-column (jdos) or the same format (pjdos) ASCII
 files: energy grid [eV], jdos/pjdos [1/eV].
